## height
# The yard is FLAT — every cell authored, so the smoothing pass cannot invent a staircase. Two
# things stand above it and they are the chapter's first platforming lesson (LOOT.md, {{LOOT_YARD}}):
#
#   x2-6, z3-6 at 6   the workshop: a solid three-cell block with a walkable paved top. It is
#                     TERRAIN, not a stamp — a `ladder_house` stamp stood here and its roof was
#                     solid, so there was nothing to climb onto. In a voxel world a building is
#                     geometry (WORLD.md §1), and the examines that used to be inside it are on
#                     its south face: the door at 4,7 and the bench at 6,7.
#   x2-3, z7-11       the ladder against its west wall, one voxel a cell, so walking gets you up.
#   x9-12, z6 at 6    the eaves of Hart's house, with the tin nailed under them.
#
# Between the roof's east edge (x6) and the eaves (x9) there are TWO cells of nothing at ground
# level. The body leaves the roof at x=6.76 and has to land at x>=9.24: 2.48 cells. A running jump
# carries 3.6 cells (7.2 cells/s for the 0.5 s a 1.25-cell apex is airborne) and a standing one
# 2.2 — so the run clears it and the walk does not, which is exactly what LOOT.md asks for. If any
# of those five numbers in voxfield.cpp changes, this gap has to be re-measured; the chapter
# play-test asserts both halves of it and will say so.
